# A language server in a child process

Most languages already have a language server: a program that answers an editor's questions about the language over the Language Server Protocol, LSP for short. Rust has rust-analyzer, Python has Pyright, Go has gopls, and TypeScript 7 has `tsc --lsp`. Loom does not rebuild those servers. A language's package starts its server as a child process and asks it about the composed code, the same way an editor would ask about a file on disk.

`LanguageServerProcess` is that bridge, and nothing in it knows which language it serves. It starts a server, keeps the files it is given open in the server, and sends the server requests. TypeScript 7 is the first package to use it, and the package for any other language reuses it unchanged.

## What the bridge offers

A language server answers only about files it knows, and a composed file exists nowhere on disk, or exists there as an older tangle. So the bridge sends each file's text to the server as an open document, which the server reads in place of the disk. `keepOpen` takes the whole set of files that should be open, each an `OpenFile` with its path and text. It opens the files new to the set, sends the new text of the files that changed, and closes the files no longer in it. Every call names the full set, so the caller never tracks what the server has already seen.

`request` sends one request and resolves with the server's answer. `alive` says whether the server is still running, so its owner can decide whether to start another. `uptime` says how long the server ran, until now or until it stopped, so its owner can tell a server that ran for a while from one that failed as soon as it started. `dispose` asks the server to shut down and stops the process.

=>

export interface OpenFile {
  readonly path: string
  readonly text: string
}

export interface LanguageServerProcess {
  readonly keepOpen: (files: ReadonlyArray<OpenFile>) => void
  readonly request: <A>(method: string, params: unknown) => Promise<A>
  readonly alive: () => boolean
  readonly uptime: () => number
  readonly dispose: () => void
}

~

A request fails with `LanguageServerFailed` when the server could not start, or has stopped, or stopped while the request waited, or answered the request with an error of its own. It names the command, so the editor's log says which server failed, and it carries the underlying cause when there is one.

The failure says why in its `reason`, a `FailureReason` of five cases, because each sends the person somewhere different. `CouldNotStart` is a server whose process never ran, and it carries the system's own words, such as `ENOENT`. Some causes last: an executable that is missing, may not be run, or was built for another machine, which starting it again cannot cure. `lasting` marks those. Others pass, such as a system briefly out of processes or open files, and a working folder that is gone, which the failure names as such rather than blaming the executable. `NotRunning` is a server that ran and has stopped. `ended` says how its process ended, with an exit code or a signal such as `SIGKILL`, and when the server wrote why it stopped, `said` carries those words, so a crash names its cause. `Unreachable` is a server that started but that Loom cannot speak to, because `vscode-jsonrpc`, the module that speaks the protocol, did not load. The fix is then in Loom's own install, not in the server, so the failure says so. `Answered` is a server that answered the request with an error of its own. Such a server is still running, and saying it is not running would send the person looking for a crash that never happened, so the failure repeats the server's words. `Refused` is a server that answered `initialize` with an error, such as a configuration it cannot read. The server's words are then the only clue to what to fix, so the failure repeats them too.

=>

export type FailureReason = Data.TaggedEnum<{
  CouldNotStart: { readonly text: string; readonly lasting: boolean }
  NotRunning: { readonly ended: Option.Option<string>; readonly said: Option.Option<string> }
  Unreachable: { readonly text: string }
  Answered: { readonly text: string }
  Refused: { readonly text: string }
}>

export const FailureReason = Data.taggedEnum<FailureReason>()

export const messageOf = (failure: unknown): string => String((failure as Error)?.message ?? failure)

export class LanguageServerFailed extends Data.TaggedError('LanguageServerFailed')<{
  readonly command: string
  readonly reason: FailureReason
  readonly cause?: unknown
}> {
  get message(): string {
    const server = `the ${this.command} language server`
    return FailureReason.$match(this.reason, {
      CouldNotStart: ({ text }) => `${server} could not start: ${text}`,
      NotRunning: ({ ended, said }) =>
        `${server} is not running` +
        Option.match(ended, { onNone: () => '', onSome: (how) => ` (${how})` }) +
        Option.match(said, { onNone: () => '', onSome: (last) => `; it last said: ${last}` }),
      Unreachable: ({ text }) => `Loom cannot speak to ${server}: ${text}`,
      Answered: ({ text }) => `${server} answered: ${text}`,
      Refused: ({ text }) => `${server} refused to start: ${text}`,
    })
  }
}

## Starting the server

`spawnLanguageServer` starts the server from a command and its arguments, in the folder the server should treat as its workspace, and speaks to it over the process's standard input and output with `vscode-jsonrpc`. It tells the server the language of the files it will open, and what the client can do, as LSP's `initialize` request asks.

The bridge mirrors a running process, so it holds state that the process changes under it: whether the process still runs, and which files it holds open. That state lives in the bridge's own closure and nowhere else, because the process is the one thing outside the program that the program must keep pace with. Everything the bridge hands out, a request's answer or a file's status, is computed from that state at the moment it is asked for.

A process can fail to start, because its command is missing, or cannot be executed, or its folder does not exist. Node reports that failure as an `error` event on the child, after the call that started it has returned. Writing to such a child would fail as well, and an unhandled failure ends the whole language server, which hosts every other language too. So the bridge waits for the child's `spawn` event, which says the process truly started, before it builds a connection or writes a byte. `spawned` is the promise of that event, and it fails with `LanguageServerFailed` on an `error` event instead, its reason built by `couldNotStart`. Node reports a working folder that does not exist under the executable's name, so `couldNotStart` checks the folder itself first. Otherwise it keeps the system's words and marks the cause `lasting` when its code is one of `lastingCodes`. A child that started can still close its input later, so the bridge listens for errors on that stream too and takes them as the process stopping. `stopped` runs once, whenever the bridge gives up on the server: when the process exits or fails, when its input breaks, or when the connection reports an error, such as a message it cannot read. It records the moment, disposes the connection, which fails every request still waiting for an answer, and stops the process. A process can outlive a broken connection, and stopping it there keeps a server the bridge can no longer talk to from running on unseen.

A crashing server explains itself only on its standard error, and it states the reason first, then the detail. A Go program such as TypeScript 7 writes a header, `panic:`, `fatal error:` or the name of a signal such as `SIGSEGV:`, then the stack of every goroutine, often more than ten kilobytes of it. A server running on Node or Bun writes the error, such as `TypeError: …`, then its stack, then the runtime's own banner, such as `Node.js v26.0.0`. So the bridge reads the standard error line by line as it arrives, and keeps `header`, the latest line that opens with one of those headers, however long the dump that follows. It also keeps the last few kilobytes, in case no header ever comes. `lastWords` gives the newest header: the line still being written, when it opens with one, or else the latest complete one. Without a header it gives the last line written that is not a runtime's banner. A server can log a harmless `Error:` line long before it dies without a word, killed for the memory it took, so a failure also says how the process ended, which `exit` reports and `ended` keeps, and the person can weigh the words against the death. The process's streams close a moment after it exits, so `closed` is the promise of that, and a failure waits for it, half a second at most, before it reads the words.

=>

const lastingCodes: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set(['ENOENT', 'EACCES', 'ENOEXEC', 'EBADARCH'])

export const spawnLanguageServer = (
  command: string,
  args: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  workspace: string,
  languageId: string,
  capabilities: object,
): LanguageServerProcess => {
  const state = {
    running: true,
    disposed: false,
    startedAt: Date.now(),
    stoppedAt: 0,
    stderr: '',
    partial: '',
    header: Option.none<string>(),
    ended: Option.none<string>(),
  }
  const couldNotStart = (cause: unknown): FailureReason =>
    existsSync(workspace)
      ? FailureReason.CouldNotStart({
          text: messageOf(cause),
          lasting: lastingCodes.has(String((cause as { readonly code?: unknown })?.code)),
        })
      : FailureReason.CouldNotStart({
          text: `its working folder ${workspace} does not exist`,
          lasting: false,
        })
  const child = spawn(command, [...args], { cwd: workspace, stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'pipe'] })
  const stopped = (): void => {
    if (!state.running) return
    state.running = false
    state.stoppedAt = Date.now()
    void connected.then((connection) => connection.dispose()).catch(() => undefined)
    child.kill()
  }
  const spawned = new Promise<void>((resolve, reject) => {
    child.once('spawn', () => resolve())
    child.once('error', (cause) =>
      reject(new LanguageServerFailed({ command, reason: couldNotStart(cause), cause })),
    )
  })
  child.on('exit', (code, signal) => {
    state.ended = Option.orElse(
      Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(signal), (name) => `killed by ${name}`),
      () => Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(code), (exit) => `exited with code ${exit}`),
    )
    stopped()
  })
  child.on('error', stopped)
  child.stdin.on('error', stopped)
  child.stderr.on('error', stopped)
  const closed = new Promise<void>((resolve) => child.once('close', () => resolve()))

  const crashHeader = /^(panic:|fatal error:|SIG[A-Z]+:|(?:[A-Z][A-Za-z]*)?[Ee]rror: )/
  const runtimeBanner = /^(Node\.js|Bun) v\d/
  const linesOf = (text: string): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
    Array.filter(
      Array.map(text.split('\n'), (line) => line.trim()),
      (line) => line !== '',
    )

  child.stderr.on('data', (chunk) => {
    const lines = (state.partial + String(chunk)).split('\n')
    state.partial = Option.getOrElse(Array.last(lines), () => '')
    state.header = Option.orElse(
      Array.findLast(linesOf(Array.dropRight(lines, 1).join('\n')), (line) => crashHeader.test(line)),
      () => state.header,
    )
    state.stderr = (state.stderr + String(chunk)).slice(-8192)
  })

  const lastWords = (): Option.Option<string> =>
    pipe(
      Option.filter(Option.some(state.partial.trim()), (line) => crashHeader.test(line)),
      Option.orElse(() => state.header),
      Option.orElse(() => Array.findLast(linesOf(state.stderr), (line) => !runtimeBanner.test(line))),
      Option.map((line) => line.slice(0, 300)),
    )

~

The bridge imports `vscode-jsonrpc` only when it starts a server. It names the module `vscode-jsonrpc/node.js`, with the extension. The package declares no map of its entry points, so Node resolves a path inside it only as written, and `vscode-jsonrpc/node` names no file to Node. Bun resolves either form, but the VS Code extension and the npm command run the language server on Node. A workspace that never runs a language server never loads the package, and the service itself loads without it, the way it loads without Prettier until it formats. `loaded` is the promise of the module, and `connected` the promise of the connection, built once the process has started and the module has arrived. A module that fails to load makes `loaded` fail as `Unreachable`, with the loader's own message, such as the module it could not find. The connection reports its own failures through `onError` and `onClose`, and the bridge takes either as the server stopping.

Writing to a server that has died fails, and when the write was a request, `vscode-jsonrpc` reports that failure twice. It fails the request for its caller, then throws the same failure again from inside the library, where no code can handle it. Node ends the whole process for such a failure, and the language server dies with every language it hosts. The warm-up makes this likely rather than rare. It queues megabytes of open documents, the server dies while it reads them, and the request behind them is written to a closed pipe.

So the bridge hands `vscode-jsonrpc` a writer whose `write` never fails. `guardedWriter` passes each message to the real writer and takes a failed write as the server stopping. Stopping disposes the connection, which fails every waiting request, so the request's caller still hears the failure, once, as the server not running.

=>

  const guardedWriter = (writer: MessageWriter): MessageWriter => ({
    write: (message) => writer.write(message).catch(() => stopped()),
    get onError() {
      return writer.onError
    },
    get onClose() {
      return writer.onClose
    },
    end: () => writer.end(),
    dispose: () => writer.dispose(),
  })

~

A server may receive nothing before it has answered `initialize`. So the bridge keeps `ready`, the promise of that answer, and sends every later message only once `ready` settles. Each message is attached to `ready` in the order the caller sent it, and a promise runs its callbacks in the order they were attached, so the server receives them in that order too. An open document always reaches the server before a request about it.

`connected` and `ready` each get a handler of their own. Every caller attaches to them as well and hears the failure on its own promise, but a promise that fails with no handler at all would be reported as unhandled, and the runtime would end the process for it. The handler on `ready` also calls `stopped`. A server that refused `initialize` but kept running can answer nothing, so the bridge treats it as stopped, and its owner's restart rules apply to it as to a crash.

=>

  const loaded: Promise<Rpc> = spawned.then(() =>
    import('vscode-jsonrpc/node.js').catch((cause: unknown) =>
      Promise.reject(
        new LanguageServerFailed({
          command,
          reason: FailureReason.Unreachable({ text: messageOf(cause) }),
          cause,
        }),
      ),
    ),
  )
  const connected: Promise<MessageConnection> = loaded.then((rpc) => {
    const connection = rpc.createMessageConnection(
      new rpc.StreamMessageReader(child.stdout),
      guardedWriter(new rpc.StreamMessageWriter(child.stdin)),
    )
    connection.onError(stopped)
    connection.onClose(stopped)
    connection.onRequest(() => null)
    connection.listen()
    return connection
  })

  const ready: Promise<MessageConnection> = connected.then((connection) =>
    connection
      .sendRequest('initialize', {
        processId: process.pid,
        rootUri: URI.file(workspace).toString(),
        workspaceFolders: [{ uri: URI.file(workspace).toString(), name: basename(workspace) }],
        capabilities,
      })
      .then(() => connection.sendNotification('initialized', {}))
      .then(() => connection),
  )
  connected.catch(() => undefined)
  ready.catch(() => stopped())

~

`keepOpen` compares the set it is given with `opened`, the text and version of every file the server holds open, and sorts the difference three ways. `closing` holds the files the server holds but the set leaves out, and each is closed with `didClose`. `opening` holds the files new to the server, and each is sent with `didOpen` at version one. `changing` holds the files whose text differs from what the server holds, and each is sent whole with `didChange`, under the next version. `opened` changes at once, while the messages wait for `ready`, so a second call before the server answers compares with what the first call sent.

=>

  const opened = new Map<string, { readonly text: string; readonly version: number }>()
  const uriOf = (path: string): string => URI.file(path).toString()
  const send = (method: string, params: unknown): void =>
    void ready.then((connection) => connection.sendNotification(method, params)).catch(() => undefined)

  const keepOpen = (files: ReadonlyArray<OpenFile>): void => {
    const wanted = new Set(Array.map(files, (file) => file.path))
    const closing = Array.filter(Array.fromIterable(opened.keys()), (path) => !wanted.has(path))
    const opening = Array.filter(files, (file) => !opened.has(file.path))
    const changing = Array.filter(files, (file) =>
      Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(opened.get(file.path)), {
        onNone: () => false,
        onSome: (held) => held.text !== file.text,
      }),
    )
    closing.forEach((path) => {
      opened.delete(path)
      send('textDocument/didClose', { textDocument: { uri: uriOf(path) } })
    })
    opening.forEach((file) => {
      opened.set(file.path, { text: file.text, version: 1 })
      send('textDocument/didOpen', {
        textDocument: { uri: uriOf(file.path), languageId, version: 1, text: file.text },
      })
    })
    changing.forEach((file) => {
      const version = (opened.get(file.path)?.version ?? 0) + 1
      opened.set(file.path, { text: file.text, version })
      send('textDocument/didChange', {
        textDocument: { uri: uriOf(file.path), version },
        contentChanges: [{ text: file.text }],
      })
    })
  }

~

`request` waits for `ready` like every other message, then sends the request. A server that is no longer running rejects the request at once, as `NotRunning` with its last words. `asked` sends the request inside the callback that `ready` runs, not a step later, so the request keeps its place among the messages attached to `ready`. A `sendRequest` that throws, on a connection already closed, becomes a failure like any other.

A failure needs one more look before it gets its reason, because `vscode-jsonrpc` reports two different events the same way. A server's error answer arrives as a `ResponseError` with the code the server sent. But `vscode-jsonrpc` also fails a request with a `ResponseError` of its own when the trouble is on the client's side, under codes it reserves for itself: a message it could not write, a message it could not read, a connection that is not active, and a request still waiting when the connection was disposed. That last one is what a server that crashes mid-request leaves behind. `answerOf` takes a `ResponseError` as the server's words only when its code is none of `clientCodes`, those four. Anything else, a `ResponseError` under one of them or a closed connection's `ConnectionError`, means the server stopped.

`failedWith` turns a failure into `LanguageServerFailed`. A failure that already is one, because the server never started or `vscode-jsonrpc` never loaded, passes through with the reason it carries. Otherwise, when `answerOf` finds the server's words, `heard` names the case they belong to. A failure of the request itself is `Answered`. A failure of `ready` is `Refused`, since the only request the server answered then was `initialize`. A failure without the server's words is `NotRunning`, and `notRunning` waits for the server's streams to close before it reads how the process ended and the server's last words.

=>

  const clientCodes = (rpc: Rpc): ReadonlySet<number> =>
    new Set([
      rpc.ErrorCodes.MessageWriteError,
      rpc.ErrorCodes.MessageReadError,
      rpc.ErrorCodes.ConnectionInactive,
      rpc.ErrorCodes.PendingResponseRejected,
    ])

  const answerOf = (rpc: Rpc, cause: unknown): Option.Option<string> =>
    cause instanceof rpc.ResponseError && !clientCodes(rpc).has(cause.code)
      ? Option.some(cause.message)
      : Option.none()

  const elapsed = (ms: number): Promise<void> => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms))

  const notRunning = (): Promise<FailureReason> =>
    Promise.race([closed, elapsed(500)]).then(() =>
      FailureReason.NotRunning({ ended: state.ended, said: lastWords() }),
    )

  const failedWith =
    (heard: (text: string) => FailureReason) =>
    (cause: unknown): Promise<never> =>
      cause instanceof LanguageServerFailed
        ? Promise.reject(cause)
        : loaded
            .then((rpc) =>
              Option.match(answerOf(rpc, cause), {
                onSome: (text) => Promise.resolve(heard(text)),
                onNone: notRunning,
              }),
            )
            .then((reason) => Promise.reject(new LanguageServerFailed({ command, reason, cause })))

  const asked = <A>(connection: MessageConnection, method: string, params: unknown): Promise<A> =>
    Result.match(
      Result.try(() => connection.sendRequest<A>(method, params)),
      {
        onSuccess: (answer) => answer.catch(failedWith((text) => FailureReason.Answered({ text }))),
        onFailure: failedWith((text) => FailureReason.Answered({ text })),
      },
    )

~

`dispose` runs once. A second call does nothing, so a server shared by several owners is shut down by the first. It asks the server to shut down, as LSP expects, then stops the process. A server that hangs would never answer the shutdown request, so the bridge waits for it two seconds at most, then stops the process regardless.

=>

  const shutdownWait = 2000

  return {
    keepOpen,
    request: <A>(method: string, params: unknown): Promise<A> =>
      state.running
        ? ready.then(
            (connection) => asked<A>(connection, method, params),
            failedWith((text) => FailureReason.Refused({ text })),
          )
        : Promise.reject(
            new LanguageServerFailed({
              command,
              reason: FailureReason.NotRunning({ ended: state.ended, said: lastWords() }),
            }),
          ),
    alive: () => state.running,
    uptime: () => (state.running ? Date.now() : state.stoppedAt) - state.startedAt,
    dispose: () => {
      if (state.disposed) return
      state.disposed = true
      const shutdown = ready
        .then((connection) =>
          connection.sendRequest('shutdown').then(() => connection.sendNotification('exit')),
        )
        .catch(() => undefined)
      void Promise.race([shutdown, elapsed(shutdownWait)]).finally(() => {
        stopped()
        child.kill()
      })
    },
  }
}

## What the bridge draws on

Node's `child_process` starts the server, and `path` names its workspace folder. `vscode-jsonrpc` frames the messages on the process's standard streams and pairs each answer with its request. It is a dependency the service imports at run time rather than bundles, for the reason the chapter on bundling the service gives. Only its types are named at the top, the `MessageConnection`, the `MessageWriter` and `Rpc`, the shape of the whole module, since the module itself arrives only when a server starts. `URI` turns a path into the `file:` address the protocol names a document by. Effect's `Data` builds the tagged `LanguageServerFailed`, `Result` catches a request that throws as it is sent, and `Array`, `Option` and `pipe` sort the files the caller wants open and pick a crashed server's last words.

=>

import { Array, Data, Option, pipe, Result } from 'effect'
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process'
import { existsSync } from 'node:fs'
import { basename } from 'node:path'
import type { MessageConnection, MessageWriter } from 'vscode-jsonrpc/node.js'
import { URI } from 'vscode-uri'

type Rpc = typeof import('vscode-jsonrpc/node.js')

## The module {Tangle}[src/LanguageServerProcess.ts]

=>

::[What the bridge draws on]

::[What the bridge offers]

::[Starting the server]

## A stand-in language server

The bridge is tested against a language server small enough to read. `standInServer` writes one to a scratch folder as an executable script with no dependencies. It frames LSP messages on its standard streams by hand, answers `initialize`, a hover and a pulled diagnostic, and writes one line to its log for every message it receives, so a case can read back exactly what the bridge sent and in what order. It writes `started` and its process id to the log when it starts, so a case can count how many processes ran and check whether one still runs.

A few file names make it misbehave, the way a real server might on some input. Any message about a file whose name holds `crash`, other than closing it, makes it exit at once, so a file opened at start ends the server as soon as it starts. A hover on a file whose name holds `boom` makes it exit then, after it has run for a while. Either way it first writes to its standard error what a crashing Go program writes: a `panic:` line naming the file, then a stack longer than the eight kilobytes the bridge keeps. A hover on a file whose name holds `throw` makes it exit the way a server on Node does, writing a `TypeError:` line, a few lines of stack, and Node's banner last. A hover on a file whose name holds `starve` makes it write a harmless `error:` line, then `fatal error: runtime: out of memory` with no line end after it, and exit with code 2. A hover on a file whose name holds `garble` makes it answer with a message that is not JSON, and keep running. A hover on a file whose name holds `refuse` makes it answer with an error, `no project here`, and keep running, the way a real server refuses a question it cannot answer. A hover on a file whose name holds `slow` is answered only after a third of a second, and the stand-in writes `answered` and the file's name to its log when it answers, so a case can check what reached the server while the question waited. Started with the argument `--refuse`, it answers `initialize` itself with an error, `cannot read tsconfig.json`, the way a real server refuses a configuration it cannot read.

The stand-in is written with plain loops and string handling. It is a fixture that stands in for a foreign program, not code of Loom's, and it must run alone, with nothing to import.

=>

import { chmodSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, realpathSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os'
import { join } from 'node:path'

export interface StandIn {
  readonly command: string
  readonly log: string
  readonly folder: string
  readonly lines: () => ReadonlyArray<string>
}

const standInSource = (log: string): string => `#!/usr/bin/env bun
const { appendFileSync } = require('node:fs')
const log = (line) => appendFileSync(${JSON.stringify(log)}, line + '\\n')
log('started ' + process.pid)
let buffer = Buffer.alloc(0)
const write = (message) => {
  const body = JSON.stringify({ jsonrpc: '2.0', ...message })
  process.stdout.write('Content-Length: ' + Buffer.byteLength(body) + '\\r\\n\\r\\n' + body)
}
const reply = (id, result) => write({ id, result })
const handle = (message) => {
  const uri = message.params?.textDocument?.uri ?? ''
  const version = message.params?.textDocument?.version
  log(message.method + (uri ? ' ' + uri.split('/').pop() : '') + (version ? ' v' + version : ''))
  const name = uri.split('/').pop()
  const crash = () => {
    const stack = 'main.handle(...)\\n\\t/stand-in/main.go:1 +0x1d\\n'.repeat(400)
    process.stderr.write('panic: stand-in crashed on ' + name + '\\n\\ngoroutine 1 [running]:\\n' + stack, () => process.exit(1))
  }
  const throwLikeNode = () => {
    const stack = '    at handle (/stand-in/server.js:1:1)\\n'.repeat(4)
    process.stderr.write('TypeError: stand-in threw on ' + name + '\\n' + stack + '\\nNode.js v26.0.0\\n', () => process.exit(1))
  }
  if (uri.includes('crash') && message.method !== 'textDocument/didClose') return crash()
  if (uri.includes('boom') && message.method === 'textDocument/hover') return crash()
  if (uri.includes('throw') && message.method === 'textDocument/hover') return throwLikeNode()
  if (uri.includes('starve') && message.method === 'textDocument/hover') {
    return process.stderr.write('error: config warning\\nfatal error: runtime: out of memory', () => process.exit(2))
  }
  if (uri.includes('garble') && message.method === 'textDocument/hover') {
    return process.stdout.write('Content-Length: 9\\r\\n\\r\\nnot json!')
  }
  if (message.id === undefined) return
  if (uri.includes('refuse') && message.method === 'textDocument/hover') {
    return write({ id: message.id, error: { code: -32803, message: 'no project here' } })
  }
  if (message.method === 'initialize' && process.argv.includes('--refuse')) {
    return write({ id: message.id, error: { code: -32603, message: 'cannot read tsconfig.json' } })
  }
  if (message.method === 'initialize') return reply(message.id, { capabilities: {} })
  if (uri.includes('slow') && message.method === 'textDocument/hover') {
    return setTimeout(() => {
      log('answered ' + uri.split('/').pop())
      reply(message.id, { contents: 'stand-in' })
    }, 300)
  }
  if (message.method === 'textDocument/hover') return reply(message.id, { contents: 'stand-in' })
  if (message.method === 'textDocument/diagnostic') return reply(message.id, { kind: 'full', items: [] })
  return reply(message.id, null)
}
process.stdin.on('data', (chunk) => {
  buffer = Buffer.concat([buffer, chunk])
  for (;;) {
    const end = buffer.indexOf('\\r\\n\\r\\n')
    if (end < 0) return
    const length = Number(/Content-Length: (\\d+)/i.exec(buffer.subarray(0, end).toString())[1])
    if (buffer.length < end + 4 + length) return
    const body = buffer.subarray(end + 4, end + 4 + length).toString()
    buffer = buffer.subarray(end + 4 + length)
    handle(JSON.parse(body))
  }
})
`

export const standInServer = (): StandIn => {
  const folder = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-stand-in-')))
  const command = join(folder, 'stand-in-server')
  const log = join(folder, 'log')
  writeFileSync(log, '')
  writeFileSync(command, standInSource(log))
  chmodSync(command, 0o755)
  return {
    command,
    log,
    folder,
    lines: () => readFileSync(log, 'utf8').split('\n').filter((line) => line !== ''),
  }
}

export const settled = (ms = 300): Promise<void> => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms))

export const startedIn = (lines: ReadonlyArray<string>): ReadonlyArray<number> =>
  lines.filter((line) => line.startsWith('started ')).map((line) => Number(line.split(' ')[1]))

export const running = (pid: number): boolean => {
  try {
    process.kill(pid, 0)
    return true
  } catch {
    return false
  }
}

## The stand-in module {Tangle}[test/StandInServer.ts]

=>

::[A stand-in language server]

## Testing the bridge

These cases drive the bridge against the stand-in server, and against commands that cannot start.

The first case checks what `keepOpen` sends and in what order. It opens `a.ts`, then changes it and opens `b.ts`, then leaves only `b.ts`. Within a call the bridge opens new files before it changes old ones, so the server receives `a.ts` at version one, `b.ts` at version one, `a.ts` at version two, and a close for `a.ts`. A request sent last reaches the server after all of them.

The next cases check that a server which cannot start never takes the host down. A command that does not exist, a file that cannot be executed, a folder that does not exist, and a server that exits as soon as it starts each leave the bridge not alive, and a request fails with `LanguageServerFailed` instead of crashing the test process. A command that does not exist is reported as one that could not start, with the system's reason. A server that answers with a message that is not JSON is stopped, and its process no longer runs.

The next cases check what a failed request says. A server that answers a hover with an error keeps running, and the failure repeats the server's words. A server that refuses `initialize` is reported as having refused to start, with its words. A server that crashes while a hover waits is reported as not running, never as having answered, although `vscode-jsonrpc` fails the waiting request with an error of its own. The failure says how the process ended and repeats the `panic:` line the server wrote before its long stack. For a server that dies the way Node does, it repeats the error line rather than Node's banner, and of two header lines it repeats the newer, even when that one has no line end. A folder that does not exist is reported as the missing folder it is. A request sent before a document is opened reaches the server first. `dispose` stops the process, and a second call does nothing.

The last two cases run the bridge under Node, the runtime the VS Code extension and the npm command use. The first starts Node on a small script that imports the bridge's own source, which Node runs by stripping its types, and has the bridge ask the stand-in for a hover. The case passes only if the hover comes back, so a module name Node cannot resolve fails it. A second case under Node floods the server with open documents, the first of them named `crash` so the server dies while it reads the rest, and then sends a request behind them. It passes only if Node reports the request failed and keeps running, which is what the guarded writer is for. A machine without Node skips both.

=>

import { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'
import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process'
import { join } from 'node:path'
import { writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { LanguageServerFailed, spawnLanguageServer } from '../src/LanguageServerProcess'
import { running, settled, standInServer, startedIn } from './StandInServer'

describe('LanguageServerProcess keeps files open and sends requests in order', () => {
  it('opens, changes and closes files, and sends a request after them', async () => {
    const stand = standInServer()
    const server = spawnLanguageServer(stand.command, [], stand.folder, 'typescript', {})
    server.keepOpen([{ path: join(stand.folder, 'a.ts'), text: 'one' }])
    server.keepOpen([
      { path: join(stand.folder, 'a.ts'), text: 'two' },
      { path: join(stand.folder, 'b.ts'), text: 'three' },
    ])
    server.keepOpen([{ path: join(stand.folder, 'b.ts'), text: 'three' }])
    const hover = await server.request<{ readonly contents: string }>('textDocument/hover', {
      textDocument: { uri: `file://${join(stand.folder, 'b.ts')}` },
      position: { line: 0, character: 0 },
    })
    expect(hover.contents).toBe('stand-in')
    expect(stand.lines().slice(1)).toEqual([
      'initialize',
      'initialized',
      'textDocument/didOpen a.ts v1',
      'textDocument/didOpen b.ts v1',
      'textDocument/didChange a.ts v2',
      'textDocument/didClose a.ts',
      'textDocument/hover b.ts',
    ])
    server.dispose()
  })
})

describe('LanguageServerProcess never takes the host down when a server cannot start', () => {
  const unstartable = async (command: string, folder: string) => {
    const server = spawnLanguageServer(command, [], folder, 'typescript', {})
    server.keepOpen([{ path: join(folder, 'a.ts'), text: 'one' }])
    const failure = await server.request('textDocument/hover', {}).then(
      () => undefined,
      (error: unknown) => error,
    )
    await settled()
    return { failure, alive: server.alive() }
  }

  it('reports a command that does not exist', async () => {
    const stand = standInServer()
    const { failure, alive } = await unstartable(join(stand.folder, 'missing'), stand.folder)
    expect(failure).toBeInstanceOf(LanguageServerFailed)
    expect((failure as LanguageServerFailed).message).toContain('could not start')
    expect((failure as LanguageServerFailed).message).toContain('ENOENT')
    expect(alive).toBe(false)
  })

  it('reports a file that cannot be executed', async () => {
    const stand = standInServer()
    const plain = join(stand.folder, 'plain')
    writeFileSync(plain, 'not a program')
    const { failure, alive } = await unstartable(plain, stand.folder)
    expect(failure).toBeInstanceOf(LanguageServerFailed)
    expect(alive).toBe(false)
  })

  it('reports a folder that does not exist', async () => {
    const stand = standInServer()
    const { failure, alive } = await unstartable(stand.command, join(stand.folder, 'gone'))
    expect(failure).toBeInstanceOf(LanguageServerFailed)
    expect((failure as LanguageServerFailed).message).toContain('working folder')
    expect((failure as LanguageServerFailed).message).toContain('does not exist')
    expect(alive).toBe(false)
  })

  it('reports a server that exits as soon as it starts', async () => {
    const stand = standInServer()
    const quitter = join(stand.folder, 'quitter')
    writeFileSync(quitter, '#!/bin/sh\nexit 3\n', { mode: 0o755 })
    const { failure, alive } = await unstartable(quitter, stand.folder)
    expect(failure).toBeInstanceOf(LanguageServerFailed)
    expect(alive).toBe(false)
  })

  it('stops a server that answers with a message it cannot read', async () => {
    const stand = standInServer()
    const server = spawnLanguageServer(stand.command, [], stand.folder, 'typescript', {})
    const failure = await server
      .request('textDocument/hover', { textDocument: { uri: `file://${join(stand.folder, 'garble.ts')}` } })
      .then(
        () => undefined,
        (error: unknown) => error,
      )
    await settled()
    expect(failure).toBeInstanceOf(LanguageServerFailed)
    expect(server.alive()).toBe(false)
    expect(startedIn(stand.lines()).every((pid) => !running(pid))).toBe(true)
  })
})

describe('LanguageServerProcess tells an error answer from a crash', () => {
  const hoverFailure = async (file: string, args: ReadonlyArray<string> = []) => {
    const stand = standInServer()
    const server = spawnLanguageServer(stand.command, args, stand.folder, 'typescript', {})
    const failure = await server
      .request('textDocument/hover', { textDocument: { uri: `file://${join(stand.folder, file)}` } })
      .then(
        () => undefined,
        (error: unknown) => error as LanguageServerFailed,
      )
    await settled()
    const alive = server.alive()
    server.dispose()
    return { failure, alive }
  }

  it('repeats the words of a server that answered with an error', async () => {
    const { failure, alive } = await hoverFailure('refuse.ts')
    expect(failure?.message).toContain('answered: no project here')
    expect(alive).toBe(true)
  })

  it('repeats the words of a server that refused to start', async () => {
    const { failure, alive } = await hoverFailure('a.ts', ['--refuse'])
    expect(failure?.message).toContain('refused to start: cannot read tsconfig.json')
    expect(alive).toBe(false)
  })

  it('reports a server that crashed mid-request as not running', async () => {
    const { failure, alive } = await hoverFailure('boom.ts')
    expect(failure?.message).toContain('is not running')
    expect(failure?.message).toContain('(exited with code 1)')
    expect(failure?.message).toContain('it last said: panic: stand-in crashed on boom.ts')
    expect(failure?.message).not.toContain('answered')
    expect(alive).toBe(false)
  })

  it('reports the error line of a server that dies the way Node does, not its banner', async () => {
    const { failure } = await hoverFailure('throw.ts')
    expect(failure?.message).toContain('it last said: TypeError: stand-in threw on throw.ts')
  })

  it('reports the newest header, even one written without a line end', async () => {
    const { failure } = await hoverFailure('starve.ts')
    expect(failure?.message).toContain('(exited with code 2)')
    expect(failure?.message).toContain('it last said: fatal error: runtime: out of memory')
  })

  it('sends a request before a document opened after it', async () => {
    const stand = standInServer()
    const server = spawnLanguageServer(stand.command, [], stand.folder, 'typescript', {})
    const hover = server.request('textDocument/hover', {
      textDocument: { uri: `file://${join(stand.folder, 'a.ts')}` },
      position: { line: 0, character: 0 },
    })
    server.keepOpen([{ path: join(stand.folder, 'b.ts'), text: 'b' }])
    await hover
    await settled()
    const lines = stand.lines()
    expect(lines.indexOf('textDocument/hover a.ts')).toBeLessThan(lines.indexOf('textDocument/didOpen b.ts v1'))
    server.dispose()
  })
})

describe('LanguageServerProcess shuts a server down once', () => {
  it('stops the process, and a second dispose does nothing', async () => {
    const stand = standInServer()
    const server = spawnLanguageServer(stand.command, [], stand.folder, 'typescript', {})
    await server.request('textDocument/hover', {})
    server.dispose()
    server.dispose()
    await settled()
    expect(server.alive()).toBe(false)
    expect(stand.lines().filter((line) => line === 'shutdown')).toEqual(['shutdown'])
  })
})

describe('LanguageServerProcess runs under Node', () => {
  const node = spawnSync('node', ['--version']).status === 0

  it.skipIf(!node)('starts a server and gets an answer when Node runs it', () => {
    const stand = standInServer()
    const script = join(stand.folder, 'under-node.ts')
    const bridge = join(import.meta.dir, '..', 'src', 'LanguageServerProcess.ts')
    writeFileSync(
      script,
      `import { spawnLanguageServer } from ${JSON.stringify(bridge)}\n` +
        `const server = spawnLanguageServer(${JSON.stringify(stand.command)}, [], ${JSON.stringify(stand.folder)}, 'typescript', {})\n` +
        `server.request('textDocument/hover', {}).then(\n` +
        `  (hover) => { console.log(JSON.stringify(hover)); server.dispose() },\n` +
        `  (error) => { console.log('failed: ' + error.message); server.dispose() },\n` +
        `)\n`,
    )
    const run = spawnSync('node', [script], { cwd: join(import.meta.dir, '..'), encoding: 'utf8', timeout: 20_000 })
    expect(run.stdout.trim()).toBe(JSON.stringify({ contents: 'stand-in' }))
  })

  it.skipIf(!node)('survives a server that dies while it reads a flood of documents', () => {
    const stand = standInServer()
    const script = join(stand.folder, 'flood.ts')
    const bridge = join(import.meta.dir, '..', 'src', 'LanguageServerProcess.ts')
    writeFileSync(
      script,
      `import { join } from 'node:path'\n` +
        `import { spawnLanguageServer } from ${JSON.stringify(bridge)}\n` +
        `const folder = ${JSON.stringify(stand.folder)}\n` +
        `const server = spawnLanguageServer(${JSON.stringify(stand.command)}, [], folder, 'typescript', {})\n` +
        `const big = '//'.repeat(200_000)\n` +
        `server.keepOpen([\n` +
        `  { path: join(folder, 'crash.ts'), text: 'x' },\n` +
        `  ...Array.from({ length: 40 }, (_, i) => ({ path: join(folder, 'p' + i + '.ts'), text: big })),\n` +
        `])\n` +
        `server.request('textDocument/hover', {}).then(\n` +
        `  () => console.log('answered'),\n` +
        `  (error) => console.log('failed: ' + error.message),\n` +
        `)\n` +
        `setTimeout(() => { console.log('survived'); server.dispose() }, 1500)\n`,
    )
    const run = spawnSync('node', [script], { cwd: join(import.meta.dir, '..'), encoding: 'utf8', timeout: 20_000 })
    expect(run.stdout).toContain('is not running')
    expect(run.stdout).toContain('survived')
    expect(run.status).toBe(0)
  })
})

